WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.879
You've probably seen our last two videos,
one about breaking a tube of sodium

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:12.059
under water and the next one breaking
two tubes of potassium under water.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:22.980
Now the real reason that Neil devised
his special device for breaking glass

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:29.640
tubes underwater was because for
years he's been holding a 5 gram sample

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:36.239
of caesium in his cupboard and he wanted
to see what would happen with breaking

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:38.340
that caesium underwater.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.230
Now, the caesium sample is different from
the other ones that we had because

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:48.539
that's a commercial sample which was
sealed under vacuum so there's no gas

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:52.860
at all and the other difference, which is
a physical difference, is that caesium

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.160
metal is much denser than water

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.340
so there is no way that caesium metal
itself can spontaneously float up to the

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.900
surface of our trough and for those of
you who watched their earlier videos

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:11.670
it's important to realize that the five
grams of caesium have about the same

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:16.860
number of atoms as the weights of
potassium and sodium that we were using

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.540
in the earlier experiments

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.810
so you have three experiments you can
compare.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:27.330
I should also say, like the others, this
was filmed outside but it was filmed a

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:31.829
bit later in the day so the sun was at a
different angle and you can see this

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:35.700
because as Neil started turning the
screw

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:41.610
there were some interesting ripples on
the surface from the vibrations and gave

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.840
quite nice patterns in the high-speed
video but this is not chemical this is

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:53.340
just vibrations again like the other two
as the test tube begins to break you see

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.680
some bubbles of hydrogen coming out now
this time we know its hydrogen because

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.750
there was no gas at all

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:01.810
inside the vessel

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.420
one part of the test tube you can see
shoots across the screen and ends up on

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:19.040
the left hand side close to the wall of
the vessel but that piece near the wall

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:25.340
still has a lot of caesium in it and the water
will then rush in again

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.920
it reacts with the caesium so there's
another big explosion and it's really big

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:35.450
because if you look at the side of the
plastic vessel which is pretty rigid it

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:42.590
bulges out and the surface of the water
looks like a model tank when they are

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.829
videoing a disaster movie of the storm

00:02:45.829 --> 00:02:49.940
huge waves and potassium it was just a
few ripples

00:02:50.569 --> 00:02:52.849
so a lot of energies come out.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.320
And this video is probably the best demonstration

00:02:59.329 --> 00:03:04.610
that I've ever seen that shows that
cesium is a lot more reactive than

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:11.630
potassium because we have had the
conditions very much the same with one

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:16.549
we get to reasonable reaction here we
get something where Neil was worried

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:19.730
that his plastic vessel might be ruined

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:37.100
we have also with this tank of water a sort of crude energy meter

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:43.010
the more the waves go the more energy is
released and here if you look at them

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:53.060
side-by-side, potassium: slight waves,
caesium: hurricane! of course we didn't do

00:03:53.060 --> 00:04:00.920
rubidium because Neil didn't have
rubidium stashed away and so if any of

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.550
you've got five grams of rubidium we're
always up for trying it.

